      "label":"Traversal-occurrence index"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Index of concepts defined outside every bounded context their member declares. FOUNDATIONS owns: recovered/independent/derived/hypothesis at vocabulary-of-standing; algorithmic description/informational interpretation/computational realisation at accounts-of-a-construction; Import/Import Declaration/Adoption/Adoption Premise at imports-between-members; Material Family/Family Table/Instance Premise/Added Choice/Adopting Product at foundation-to-law; Supporting Evidence of a categorical claim at the algebra's Formal fragment, superseding Evidence Class; Join's four senses—categorical join, composition boundary, join of results, relation join—at vocabulary-of-standing; Assurance Relations, Assertion Provenance, Execution Provenance, Observation Sufficiency, Impact Cover, Validity Dependency and Mandatory Gate at assurance-relations. ACTIVATION owns Current State/Destination/Transition at transitions; Construction Relation/Strategy/Composition Boundary/Feedback and Diagnosis/the four causes at construction-relation; Configuration at configuration; Acquisition/Derived Surface/deciding owner section at context-construction; Rewrite Step/Licensing Law/Exact Step/Approximate Step/Adequacy Relation/End-to-End Bound at refinement; Truth Surface/actor's account at workspace-and-an-actors-account. These are indexed names, not supplied full definitions.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":340,
          "quote":"This section indexes the concepts that a member defines outside every\nbounded context it declares. The source context of each is its owning\nmember\n([FOUNDATIONS §2 Imports between members](FOUNDATIONS.md#imports-between-members)),\nwhich the second column names in place of a bounded-context identity. A\nrow here declares no context; a context holds one of these concepts only\nby its own import, as §8 records for the concepts that REFERENCE_FRAME §1\nimports (§2; P1, P3).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/GLOSSARY.md#GLOSSARY%2F16.%20Concepts%20of%20members%20without%20a%20declared%20context",
          "start_line":333
        }
      ],
      "key":"member_owned_concept_index",
      "label":"Member-owned concepts without declared contexts"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Further member-owned index entries: EVALUATION owns Equivalence Ladder, semantic equivalence of realisations, Computational Equivalence and Categorical Equivalence at §6, explicitly distinct from Semantic Equivalence of concepts in glossary §5; Adequate Attention and Actual-Use Evidence at frame-qualification. CONFORMANCE owns Discriminator/Common Discriminators at conformance and Logical-Review Check at §5. PROFILES owns the profile import rule at §1. DESIGN_MODULE owns axiom verdict {pass, fail, not_applicable} at cross-view-axiom-evaluation and Ontology/design verdict {accepted} at gate-and-retrospective-work. UX §15 owns separate domains surface review {compliant, violation, design_module_incomplete}, replay proof {replay_passes, replay_fails}, closure obligations {closure_obligations_met, closure_unproven}, carrier admission {carrier_admitted, carrier_missing}. Results references do not themselves supply their predicates or core mappings.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":358,
          "quote":"| `Equivalence Ladder`; semantic equivalence of realisations; `Computational Equivalence`; `Categorical Equivalence` | EVALUATION | [`EVALUATION.md#6-conformance-of-realisations`](EVALUATION.md#6-conformance-of-realisations) | [`FOUNDATIONS.md#4-algebra`](FOUNDATIONS.md#4-algebra); `Semantic Equivalence` of concepts in §5 is another concept |",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/GLOSSARY.md#GLOSSARY%2F16.%20Concepts%20of%20members%20without%20a%20declared%20context",
          "start_line":358
        },
        {
          "end_line":368,
          "quote":"| carrier admission: `carrier_admitted`; `carrier_missing` | UX | [`UX.md#15-results-of-this-profile`](UX.md#15-results-of-this-profile) | [`FOUNDATIONS.md#results`](FOUNDATIONS.md#results) |",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/GLOSSARY.md#GLOSSARY%2F16.%20Concepts%20of%20members%20without%20a%20declared%20context",
          "start_line":368
        }
      ],
      "key":"member_evaluation_and_result_index",
      "label":"Member-owned evaluation concepts and result domains"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Owner-qualified senses: indeterminate means either a FOUNDATIONS core result where admissible evidence cannot decide, or an EVALUATION triage payload class, not a result. not_applicable means an EVALUATION triage payload class, a RELEASE successor disposition with bounded reason, a SPEC_METHOD lifecycle phase declared inapplicable with reason, a DESIGN_MODULE/ACTIVATION design-grounded exclusion and entity-lifecycle matrix entry, or a TRAVERSAL_OCCURRENCE judgment value. refusal/refused means REFERENCE_FRAME method-function activation/translation refusal, AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS constraint/basis refusal, TRAVERSAL_OCCURRENCE judgment decision/stop, or ACTIVATION work-contract admission refusal. gap means an a_c stop carrying a core value or a FOUNDATIONS conjunction gap with no value of its own. disposition means ACTIVATION boundary acceptance, RELEASE treatment of a predecessor claim, TICKETS obligation repricing, or a PROJECT_REFERENCE_FRAME_BASIS_TEMPLATE Coverage And Residuals planning choice for an evaluation, not coverage status. missing means absence of a closed result, under FOUNDATIONS Not performed. adoption means PRODUCT_DEFINITION Product adoption of an available profile, SPEC_METHOD adoption of a shared method lesson, or RELEASE consumer adoption of a release basis. Closed/closed means POSTING_GUIDE discussion state or TICKETS ticket/sprint status.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":399,
          "quote":"| disposition | a planning choice for an evaluation in a basis inventory, not a coverage status | `templates/PROJECT_REFERENCE_FRAME_BASIS_TEMPLATE.md`, Coverage And Residuals |",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/GLOSSARY.md#GLOSSARY%2F17.%20Labels%20with%20several%20senses",
          "start_line":399
        },
        {
          "end_line":395,
          "quote":"| `gap` | a conjunction gap, which has no value of its own | [FOUNDATIONS §4 Conjunction of results](FOUNDATIONS.md#conjunction-of-results) |",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/GLOSSARY.md#GLOSSARY%2F17.%20Labels%20with%20several%20senses",
          "start_line":395
        }
      ],
      "key":"owner_qualified_label_senses",
      "label":"Labels with several owner-qualified senses"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The glossary's own predecessor-disposition register concerns rc.2 GLOSSARY_GUIDE.md. Routes are provenance only. It claims conserved index-contract rules; method-identity terms at SPEC_METHOD §1 with O's relation at ODD §1; fourteen calculus rows; seven traversal-profile rows; eight semantic-resolution terms moved to PRODUCT_DEFINITION §5 except Semantic Import owned by FOUNDATIONS imports; eleven product-definition terms moved to PRODUCT_DEFINITION §§1,3,4,7; seven release terms at RELEASE §2; reference-frame imports from FOUNDATIONS, two specialist families at EXECUTIVE_BASELINE §11, added frame objects/core algebra/ACTIVATION refinements; build-tenancy context at PRODUCT_DEFINITION §8 with Build Tenant at SPEC_METHOD §11, three other rows at §8 and work item at TICKETS §4; eight recursive-product terms at FOUNDATIONS Product lineage objects; five assurance rows at ASSURANCE §2 and Unit Test at §15 with DESIGN_MODULE relation; eight ODD rows with ABG and F_P/F_H relations and duplicate calculus indexing; two ambiguity-governance terms at SPEC_METHOD §5; nine world-model terms at WORLD_MODEL §6 and Builder Project specialisation. These are source-authored conservation claims, not independently established conservation.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":415,
          "quote":"Rows of rc.2 GLOSSARY_GUIDE.md. Anchors are into rc.2 GLOSSARY_GUIDE.md; a\nlocus is a section of this file. Every conserved row is restated in this\nmember's own text; a route is provenance only. An index section is\nconserved when every predecessor row in it is indexed here with its\nbounded-context identity unchanged and its locus moved to the successor\nclause that owns the concept; the predecessor's owner moves are recorded\nper row.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/GLOSSARY.md#GLOSSARY%2F18.%20Source%20dispositions",
          "start_line":409
        }
      ],
      "key":"glossary_predecessor_dispositions",
      "label":"Glossary predecessor-disposition register"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Tier-1 IDENTITY member owns scoped identity, uniqueness, type, lifecycle stability, readable/opaque forms, hierarchy, engine/domain split, authority conservation, overridable defaults and publication, parsing, object/release boundary, minimum questions, examples and implementation consequences. It consumes FOUNDATIONS §2 Object, Authority and Basis without redefining them. AC-002 applies its scoped identity to every calculus identity with mapped authority/kind/value coordinates; calculus-edition identity remains owned by AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS §8, and release names/forms by RELEASE. Clauses trace P0–P6; identity is P3's commitment and authority P6's. rc.2 anchors refer to IDENTITY_METHOD.md as provenance, not an application dependency.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":20,
          "quote":"with a map of its coordinates to authority, kind and value. The identity\nof one calculus edition is\n[AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS §8](AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#8-calculus-identity), which\nthis member does not restate. Release identity, its names and its forms are\nthe release law of [RELEASE](RELEASE.md); this member states only the\nboundary between it and object identity.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/IDENTITY.md#IDENTITY",
          "start_line":15
        }
      ],
      "key":"identity_law",
      "label":"Identity law"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "F8 identity foundation-to-law table distinguishes consequences from owner-selected policy. Instance premises, not supplied by commitments, are: projects contain many kinds with runtime and domain owners; identities have readable/serialised forms read by people and parsers; projects publish releases whose versions are release coordinates. Row premise sets are: scoped/type/uniqueness P3+(i); stability P3+P4b; boundary claims P3+P6+P5+(i); readable forms P3+P1+(ii); hierarchy P3+(i),(ii); authority split P3+P6+(i); authority conservation P6+P3+P4b; defaults P3+P6; parsing P3+P1+(ii); release boundary P3+P6+(iii); minimum questions P3+P6; implementation policy P3+P1+P0. P0 is recovered, P1/P3/P6 independent, P4b derived from P0/P2, P5 derived from P0/P6 with P0's open-world premise. Choices retain owners: owning authority chooses further coordinates, kinds, readable/opaque/hybrid form and visible serialisation; runtime chooses its generation rules and defaults; domain chooses its grammar, overrides/publication and need for families; issuing authority chooses grants/decisions; profiles choose projection terms; this member chooses the general hierarchy model, grant-field scheme, seven questions and enduring-repair policy. WORLD_MODEL refines the Markov construct, REFERENCE_FRAME owns material sufficiency, FOUNDATIONS owns general authority limits, RELEASE owns release identity.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":57,
          "quote":"Instance premises,\nwhich the commitments do not supply:\n\n- (i) a governed project holds objects of many kinds, some owned by a\n  runtime or engine and some by the domain that defines them;\n- (ii) identities are written in serialised or readable forms that people\n  and parsers read;\n- (iii) a project publishes releases, whose versions are coordinates of\n  the release and not of each object.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/IDENTITY.md#IDENTITY%2F1.%20Position%2FFoundation%20to%20law",
          "start_line":49
        }
      ],
      "key":"identity_foundation_table",
      "label":"F8 premises, consequences and owned choices"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Object identity is always scoped; its minimum semantic model is authority + kind + value. At minimum it should be interpretable through those coordinates. Additional coordinates may include parent scope, revision, publication version and time.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":114,
          "quote":"Identity is always scoped. At minimum, identity should be interpretable\nthrough authority, kind and value. So the minimum semantic model is:\n\n```text\nidentity = authority + kind + value\n```\n\nThis is the scoped identity of an Object (FOUNDATIONS §2). Optional\nadditional coordinates may include parent scope, revision, publication\nversion and temporal coordinates (P3; rc.2 #1-scoped-identity).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/IDENTITY.md#IDENTITY%2F2.%20Core%20law%2FScoped%20identity",
          "start_line":105
        }
      ],
      "key":"scoped_identity",
      "label":"Scoped identity"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Engine-owned identities concern runtime-owned runs, function calls, jobs, execution frames, continuations and events. Domain-owned identities concern a derived domain's constitutional requirements, requirement families, acceptance criteria, scenarios, ADRs/design decisions and published domain assets. The domain defines its kinds, distinctions, uniqueness and parsing/reading; the runtime may transport these identities.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":217,
          "quote":"The substrate or runtime may define default identities for runtime-owned\nobjects, such as runs, function calls, jobs, execution frames,\ncontinuations and events. For these, the engine may use opaque identities and engine-local\ngeneration rules. Those rules should optimise for uniqueness, replay\nsafety and event-stream integrity. The engine should not impose\ndomain-specific semantic grammar on domain-owned objects (P3, P6;\nrc.2 #engine-owned-identity).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/IDENTITY.md#IDENTITY%2F5.%20Authority%20split%2FEngine-owned%20identity",
          "start_line":211
        },
        {
          "end_line":227,
          "quote":"Derived domains own the semantic identity grammar for their own\nconstitutional objects, such as requirements, requirement families,\nacceptance criteria, scenarios, ADRs or design decisions, and published\ndomain assets. The domain defines which identity kinds exist, how they\nare distinguished, what makes them unique, and how they are parsed or\nread. The runtime may transport those identities, but it should not\nsilently invent their meaning (P3, P6; rc.2 #domain-owned-identity).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/IDENTITY.md#IDENTITY%2F5.%20Authority%20split%2FDomain-owned%20identity",
          "start_line":221
        }
      ],
      "key":"identity_ownership_split",
      "label":"Engine-owned and domain-owned identity"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Authority is an identity-bearing semantic relation preserving owner, kind, governed subject, scope and governing basis; it is not a display label, capability string, actor identity or reachable code path. Human authority may act directly or through an explicitly admitted bounded proxy, whose actor identity remains distinct from human-authority identity.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":235,
          "quote":"Authority is an identity-bearing semantic relation, not a display label,\ncapability string, actor identity or reachable code path. Its identity\npreserves owner, kind, governed subject, scope and governing basis (P6,\nP3; rc.2 #authority-identity-and-conservation-stdo-up-004; the Authority\nobject and its kinds are FOUNDATIONS §2).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/IDENTITY.md#IDENTITY%2F6.%20Authority%20identity%20and%20conservation%20%28STDO-UP-004%29",
          "start_line":231
        }
      ],
      "key":"authority_identity",
      "label":"Authority identity"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Domain override of a bound runtime realisation's identity defaults. Defaults are starter conventions and convenience contracts; their publication and applicable surfaces belong to DESIGN_MODULE §12 realisation law. An override concerns the domain's own identity kinds and is published through domain authority.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":272,
          "quote":"Default identity formats that a bound runtime realisation publishes are\nstarter conventions and convenience contracts, not universal doctrine;\nthey are lawful only so long as derived domains may override them (P3,\nP6). What such a realisation publishes, and for which surfaces, is\nrealisation law outside this member\n([DESIGN_MODULE §12](DESIGN_MODULE.md#identity-defaults-from-a-bound-runtime-realisation)).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/IDENTITY.md#IDENTITY%2F7.%20Default%20formats%20and%20overrides%2FDerived%20domain%20overrides",
          "start_line":267
        }
      ],
      "key":"domain_identity_override",
      "label":"Domain identity override"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "IDENTITY's register of predecessor claims from rc.2 IDENTITY_METHOD.md. It claims conservation of substantive identity rules and treats core-law, authority-split, default-formats-and-overrides and examples as claim-free headings whose children are handled separately. Generalisations replace graph_function/asset_surface/graph_call with published_function/carrier_surface/function_call; graph calls/frames with function calls/execution frames; ABG-specific realisation wording with runtime wording; profile-specific F_H projection with permission for a profile's own term; Id with identity. Execution frames remain distinct from FOUNDATIONS frames. Predecessor must becomes indicative; should, may, at minimum, at most, optional, useful and suitable retain force. The defaults-publication permission moves to DESIGN_MODULE §12; identity-law conditions stay here. Release-version selector revision is expressly proposed and pending owner acceptance, while other boundary determinations are claimed unchanged and release forms remain at RELEASE §1.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":431,
          "quote":"\"Id\" is written \"identity\"; the\npredecessor's \"must\" is written in the indicative and its \"should\",\n\"may\", \"at minimum\", \"at most\", \"optional\", \"useful\" and \"suitable\" keep\ntheir force. Release version boundary: superseded, proposed and pending the product owner's acceptance.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/IDENTITY.md#IDENTITY%2F13.%20Source%20dispositions",
          "start_line":428
        }
      ],
      "key":"identity_predecessor_dispositions",
      "label":"Identity predecessor-disposition register"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Tier-3 Outcome-Driven Development profile for graph-native product authoring over a declared asset graph. Its graph-native bounded context is urn:stdo:bounded-context:graph-native-odd under the selected complete STDO basis. It owns product shape, graph assets/nodes/vectors/functions/traversals and graph/runtime worker/evaluator shorthands, without absorbing a consuming Product's domain semantics or similarly named concrete runtime carriers. It imports foundation families by reference without restating them. ODD_METHOD rc.2 routes are provenance only.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":11,
          "quote":"Tier 3. The Outcome-Driven Development profile: graph-native product\nauthoring over a declared asset graph. It imports the families that\n[§1 Foundation to law](#foundation-to-law) lists by reference under\n[PROFILES §1](PROFILES.md#1-the-boundary) and restates none of them. Inside\nits bounded context it uses its own terms freely: graph, node, asset,\nvector, edge, graph function, traversal, worksite, ledger, event log, GTL,\nABG, F_D, F_P and F_H. A route written `rc.2 #anchor` is into\n`stdo://releases/v2.5.1-rc.2/standards/ODD_METHOD.md` and is provenance\nonly (P0, P1).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/ODD.md#ODD",
          "start_line":3
        },
        {
          "end_line":62,
          "quote":"This profile owns those concepts in the graph-native bounded context\n`urn:stdo:bounded-context:graph-native-odd` under the selected complete\nSTDO basis. The context covers the ODD product shape, the GTL language\nrole, the ABG method role, graph assets, vectors, functions, traversals and\nthe graph and runtime worker and evaluator shorthands defined here. It does\nnot absorb a consuming Product's domain semantics, nor a concrete runtime's\ncarriers that happen to share a spelling (P1, P3).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/ODD.md#ODD%2F1.%20Position",
          "start_line":56
        }
      ],
      "key":"odd_profile",
      "label":"ODD profile"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "GTL, Graph Transformation Language, is the typed declarative graph-language capability role and language expression of ODD. ABG is the singular traversal-governance and runtime-truth capability role, realising the GTL program through governance, binding and runtime truth without owning domain-internal HOW. These names denote roles and a construction algebra, not required repositories, packages, vendors or implementations.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":54,
          "quote":"ODD, GTL and ABG name normative capability roles and a construction\nalgebra, not a required repository, package, vendor or concrete\nimplementation. ODD, Outcome-Driven Development, names the graph-native\nproduct-authoring profile. GTL, Graph Transformation Language, names the\nrole of the typed declarative graph language. ABG names the role of the\nsingular traversal-governance and runtime-truth authority (P1, P6).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/ODD.md#ODD%2F1.%20Position",
          "start_line":49
        }
      ],
      "key":"gtl_abg_roles",
      "label":"GTL and ABG capability roles"
    },
    {
      "conditions": ["A product adopts the ODD profile."],
      "definition": "F18 foundation-to-law table imports F1 frames/context, F2 transitions/steps/configuration, F3 results/combination, F4 work state/resumption/reuse, F6 claims/evidence/assessment/acceptance, F9 a_c, F10 constitutional chain/outcome/build tenancy, F11 change/ambiguity, F12 project-method binding, F13 work carriers and F15 release/successor conservation; imported rules and choices retain their owners. Adopted instance premises, not foundation-derived, are I1 externally authoritative selected Product outcome; I2 mutable workspace beside immutable ledger/event log; I3 bounded probabilistic constructive workers whose reports/artifacts are observations; I4 building with an installed method-product release, possibly an earlier self-release. P0 is recovered; P1/P2/P3/P4a/P6 independent; P4b derives from P0/P2; P5 derives from P0/P6. The table distinguishes derived consequences from profile and Product choices, lists consumers per row, and attributes rule decisions to their owners rather than consumers.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":111,
          "quote":"Its instance premises hold where a product is built with this profile;\nthey are adopted with it and do not follow from the foundations (P1, P6):\n\n- I1: a selected Product outcome is admitted as external authority for\n  the work;\n- I2: the work changes a mutable workspace, beside which an immutable\n  ledger and event log record the admitted work;\n- I3: bounded probabilistic workers carry out the constructive actions,\n  and their reports and artifacts are observations;\n- I4: the product is built with an installed release of a method product,\n  which may be an earlier release of the product itself.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/ODD.md#ODD%2F1.%20Position%2FFoundation%20to%20law",
          "start_line":101
        }
      ],
      "key":"odd_foundation_table",
      "label":"F18 imports, instance premises and owned choices"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The method library distinguishes mutable authoring source for a future cut from installed distributions of one complete selected immutable RC cut. Both contain method members, guides and templates. Method members are constitutional surfaces: predecessor *_METHOD.md files and current README-designated tier-1/tier-2 standards plus tier-3 profiles when selected. *_GUIDE.md guides refine method application as operational guides/application standards. *_TEMPLATE.md templates are reusable starter forms/examples and convenience artifacts only.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":274,
          "quote":"Both the authoring library and an installed distribution contain three\nclasses of document. Method members are the constitutional method\nsurfaces; in the predecessor library they are the `*_METHOD.md` members,\nand in this corpus they are the members that [README](README.md#documents)\nnames as method members: the tier 1 and tier 2 standards and, when a\nproject selects them, the tier 3 profiles. Guides (`*_GUIDE.md`) are operational guides and application standards\nthat refine application of the method. Templates (`*_TEMPLATE.md`) are\nreusable starter forms and examples and are convenience artifacts only.\nOnly method members are constitutional method surfaces (P6, P3).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/ODD.md#ODD%2F4.%20Method%20library%20classes",
