    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A vector is a lawful/admissible transition between nodes. Each declares required upstream assets, target asset, governing evaluators and gates, and output contract.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":295,
          "quote":"Vectors are the lawful transitions between nodes and are the admissible\ntransitions of the product. Each vector declares its required upstream\nassets, its target asset, its governing evaluators and gates, and its\noutput contract (P4a, P6).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/ODD.md#ODD%2F5.%20Graph%20model%2FVectors",
          "start_line":292
        }
      ],
      "key":"odd_vector",
      "label":"ODD vector"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Probabilistic compute is bounded constructive work over one declared vector or edge traversal whose worker-internal solution path is not fully determined by graph/runtime. The vector's admissible external space declares upstream assets, target/output contract, context, role/capability expectation, evaluators/gates, provenance obligation and lawful stop/hold/gap/continuation/completion states. ABG may govern, invoke, observe and classify; unconstrained internal HOW includes hidden reasoning, heuristic search, implementation strategy, tool-local execution and product-specific business choice.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":324,
          "quote":"The vector declares the admissible external traversal space: required\nupstream assets; target asset or output contract; required context; role\nor capability expectation; governing evaluators and gates; provenance\nobligation; and the lawful stop, hold, gap, continuation or completion\nstates. ABG may govern, invoke, observe and classify the traversal; it\ndoes not own the worker's hidden constructive path. Whatever the traversal\nboundary does not constrain remains worker-internal HOW: hidden LLM\nreasoning, heuristic search, domain implementation strategy, tool-local\nexecution detail and product-specific business choice (P0, P6).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/ODD.md#ODD%2F5.%20Graph%20model%2FProbabilistic%20compute",
          "start_line":316
        }
      ],
      "key":"odd_probabilistic_compute",
      "label":"ODD probabilistic compute"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "ODD-local F_P (urn:stdo:concept:graph-native-odd:f-p) is a bounded probabilistic worker turn: bounded non-human construction under declared policy narrowed to one graph traversal contract. ODD-local F_H (urn:stdo:concept:graph-native-odd:f-h) is explicit human adjudication invoked under the traversal's declared authority and ambiguity policy, with no ambient traversal, semantic or closure authority. F_D (urn:stdo:concept:graph-native-odd:f-d) is deterministic evaluation/proof over declared traversal properties; it may optimise or validate precise domain-local work without moving domain HOW into GTL, ABG or framework.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":345,
          "quote":"- `F_P` (`urn:stdo:concept:graph-native-odd:f-p`) is the shorthand for the\n  bounded probabilistic worker turn. It specialises substrate-neutral\n  lawful probabilistic processing: it keeps bounded non-human construction\n  under declared policy and narrows that meaning to one graph traversal\n  contract.\n- `F_D` (`urn:stdo:concept:graph-native-odd:f-d`) is the shorthand for\n  deterministic evaluation or proof over declared properties of the\n  traversal. F_D may optimise or validate a domain-local path where the\n  domain can make part of the work precise; it does not move domain HOW\n  into GTL, ABG or the framework.\n- `F_H` (`urn:stdo:concept:graph-native-odd:f-h`) is the shorthand for\n  explicit human adjudication invoked under the traversal's declared\n  authority and ambiguity policy. It specialises the substrate-neutral\n  human-adjudication concept and grants a human no ambient traversal,\n  semantic or closure authority.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/ODD.md#ODD%2F5.%20Graph%20model%2FProbabilistic%20compute",
          "start_line":331
        }
      ],
      "key":"odd_work_classifications",
      "label":"ODD F_P, F_D and F_H classifications"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Referenced source concepts for two explicitly declared specialisations: urn:stdo:concept:ambiguity-governance:lawful-probabilistic-processing and urn:stdo:concept:ambiguity-governance:human-adjudication, with their context owned by SPEC_METHOD §5. Only their preserved meanings stated in ODD are available here; their full owner definitions are not supplied.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":355,
          "quote":"The F_P\nspecialisation runs from\n`urn:stdo:concept:ambiguity-governance:lawful-probabilistic-processing` to\n`urn:stdo:concept:graph-native-odd:f-p`; the F_H specialisation runs from\n`urn:stdo:concept:ambiguity-governance:human-adjudication` to\n`urn:stdo:concept:graph-native-odd:f-h`. The source concepts and their\ncontext are owned by\n[SPEC_METHOD §5](SPEC_METHOD.md#5-ambiguity-governance-and-lifecycle); this\nprofile owns the targets in the graph-native ODD context (P1, P6).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/ODD.md#ODD%2F5.%20Graph%20model%2FProbabilistic%20compute",
          "start_line":347
        }
      ],
      "key":"referenced_ambiguity_governance_sources",
      "label":"Referenced probabilistic-processing and human-adjudication sources"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Graph functions are named constructive programs realising vectors. They may realise an outer vector directly, refine it into a small inner workflow, or act as reusable higher-order library functions. The outer vector remains the constitutional/callable contract. ODD binds ACTIVATION typed refinement to such functions using §11.8's cumulative carried environment as its specialised contract; this remains profile law rather than general core law.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":379,
          "quote":"Graph functions are named constructive programs that realise vectors. A\ngraph function may realise one outer vector directly, refine one outer\nvector into a small inner workflow, or act as a reusable higher-order\nlibrary function. The outer vector remains the constitutional contract;\nthe graph function is the constructive realisation of that contract (P4a,\nP0).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/ODD.md#ODD%2F5.%20Graph%20model%2FGraph%20functions",
          "start_line":374
        }
      ],
      "key":"odd_graph_function",
      "label":"ODD graph function"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Source-defined workflow: bind the current target; locate next admissible vectors; traverse one vector; materialise the next asset; prove the vector closed; continue until the selected target closes. This describes a traversal operation without creating or performing a traversal record.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":411,
          "quote":"The workflow is a traversal over the graph: bind the current target;\nlocate the next admissible vectors; traverse one vector; materialise the\nnext asset; prove the vector closed; continue until the selected target\ncloses. The graph is the declared program space of the product (P0, P5).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/ODD.md#ODD%2F5.%20Graph%20model%2FTraversal",
          "start_line":408
