        },
        {
          "end_line":340,
          "quote":"A\nrow here declares no context; a context holds one of these concepts only\nby its own import, as §8 records for the concepts that REFERENCE_FRAME §1\nimports (§2; P1, P3).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/GLOSSARY.md#GLOSSARY%2F16.%20Concepts%20of%20members%20without%20a%20declared%20context",
          "start_line":337
        },
        {
          "end_line":379,
          "quote":"Some labels name different things in different clauses. Each row below\nlocates one sense with the clause that owns it; the label's meaning at an\noccurrence is the sense its owning clause gives, and the occurrence\nqualifies the label or states its predicate where it is used. Equal\nspelling across these rows establishes no equivalence (§1; P1, P3).\nPurpose and material relation: an actor that read one sense into another\nclause reconciled the two by itself; this index points each sense to its\nowner.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/GLOSSARY.md#GLOSSARY%2F17.%20Labels%20with%20several%20senses",
